2958 #edb/ref 1077 "field-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412958 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186412876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412958 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fd3fc3a4-ac23-58a2-9227-8ac4db6385c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412959 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412959 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86034" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412959 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412959 #edb/ref 1071 "If $[a]_{n}$ denotes the residue class containing all integers congruent to $a$ modulo $n,$ then $[-21]_{6}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412959 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "06e706ec-ef55-5e4d-a1fb-e7ced3a44078" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412959 #edb/ref 1074 "We have $[a]_{6}=[b]_{6}$ if \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6).\n$$\n\nThe residue class $[-21]_{6}$ consists of the elements that are congruent to $-21$ modulo $6.$ \n\nWe can find an alternative representation for this residue class as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[-21]_{6} & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡-21\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡(-4)\\cdot 6+3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡(-4)\\cdot 0+3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)} \\\\  & =[3]_{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412960 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412960 #edb/ref 1069 "q-214477" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412960 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412960 #edb/ref 1071 "If $[a]_{n}$ denotes the residue class containing all integers congruent to $a$ modulo $n,$ then $[-26]_{6}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412960 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0c2846ce-57fa-59ad-9fe2-ab1c6ed9c653" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412960 #edb/ref 1074 "We have $[a]_{6}=[b]_{6}$ if \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6).\n$$\n\nThe residue class $[-26]_{6}$ consists of the elements that are congruent to $-26$ modulo $6.$ \n\nWe can find an alternative representation for this residue class as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[-26]_{6} & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡-26\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡(-5)\\cdot 6+4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡(-5)\\cdot 0+4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)} \\\\  & =[4]_{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412961 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412961 #edb/ref 1069 "q-78640" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412961 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412961 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- There are $3$ distinct residue classes modulo $6$\n- $[5]_{3}\\cap [1]_{3}\\ne ∅$\n- $Z=[3]_{2}\\cup [6]_{2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412961 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0f9012f2-83ba-5f6f-b553-2ea47d5d4f81" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412961 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the residue classes modulo $n$ have the following properties:\n\n- There are exactly $n$ distinct residue classes modulo $n$\n\n- Two distinct residue classes have an empty intersection: $[a]_{n}\\cap [b]_{n}=∅$ if $a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- Residue classes modulo $n$ form a partition of the set of all integers: \n\n$$\nZ=[0]_{n}\\cup [1]_{n}\\cup ⋯\\cup [n-1]_{n}\n$$\n\n- The integers modulo $n$ is defined as the set containing all distinct residue classes modulo $n$: \n\n$$\n{Z}_{n}={[0]_{n},\\,[1]_{n},\\,…,\\,[n-1]_{n}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. There are exactly $6$ distinct residue classes modulo $6.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $5≢1\\,(\\text{mod }3),$ we have that $[5]_{3}\\cap [1]_{3}=∅.$\n\n- Statement III is true. First, notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[3]_{2} & =[1+2\\cdot 1]_{2}=[1]_{2} \\\\ [6]_{2} & =[0+2\\cdot 3]_{2}=[0]_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[3]_{2}\\cup [6]_{2} & =[1]_{2}\\cup [0]_{2} \\\\  & =[0]_{2}\\cup [1]_{2} \\\\  & =Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412962 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412962 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86035" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412962 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412962 #edb/ref 1071 "If $[a]_{n}$ denotes the residue class containing all integers congruent to $a$ modulo $n,$ then $[-13]_{5}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412962 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1bd78006-ade4-5556-a33a-5b5262685fbe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412962 #edb/ref 1074 "We have $[a]_{5}=[b]_{5}$ if \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5).\n$$\n\nThe residue class $[-13]_{5}$ consists of the elements that are congruent to $-13$ modulo $5.$ \n\nWe can find an alternative representation for this residue class as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[-13]_{5} & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡-13\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡(-3)\\cdot 5+2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡(-3)\\cdot 0+2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)} \\\\  & =[2]_{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412963 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412963 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412963 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412963 #edb/ref 1069 "q-214479" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412963 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412963 #edb/ref 1071 "Select the correct options in the statements below regarding residue classes modulo $12:$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412963 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "32f1fbfd-ebfc-5a37-bf9e-b69da2b29724" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412963 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the residue classes modulo $n$ have the following properties:\n\n- There are exactly $n$ distinct residue classes modulo $n$\n\n- Two distinct residue classes have an empty intersection: $[a]_{n}\\cap [b]_{n}=∅$ if $a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- Residue classes modulo $n$ form a partition of the set of all integers: \n\n$$\nZ=[0]_{n}\\cup [1]_{n}\\cup ⋯\\cup [n-1]_{n}\n$$\n\n- The integers modulo $n$ is defined as the set containing all distinct residue classes modulo $n$: \n\n$$\n{Z}_{n}={[0]_{n},\\,[1]_{n},\\,…,\\,[n-1]_{n}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following properties:\n\n- $[0]_{12}\\cap [6]_{12}=∅.$\n\n- $Z=[0]_{12}\\cup [1]_{12}\\cup ⋯\\cup [11]_{12}.$\n\n- ${Z}_{12}={[0]_{12},[1]_{12},⋯,[11]_{12}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412964 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412964 #edb/ref 1069 "q-214450" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412964 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412964 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following integers belong to the residue class $[4]_{7}?$\n\n- $-3$\n- $6$\n- $18$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412964 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "44e36839-2675-57c4-abf9-91a0b0717003" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412964 #edb/ref 1074 "The residue class $[4]_{7}$ consists of the elements that are congruent to $4$ modulo $7.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[4]_{7} & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x=4+7k,\\,k\\in Z} \\\\  & ={4+7k\\,|\\,k\\in Z} \\\\  & ={…,\\,4-2\\cdot 7,\\,4-7,\\,4,\\,4+7,\\,4+2\\cdot 7,\\,…} \\\\  & ={…,-10,-3,4,11,18,…}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $-3$ and $18$ belong to $[4]_{7},$ while $6$ does not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412965 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412965 #edb/ref 1069 "q-78604" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412965 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412965 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following residue classes modulo $6$ conta